#include "rbtree.h"
#include "rb_alloc.h"
#include <string.h>

typedef struct rbnode {
    char *key;               /* owned copy; NULL on the nil sentinel */
    void *value;              /* owned iff value_free != NULL */
    struct rbnode *left;
    struct rbnode *right;
    struct rbnode *parent;
    enum { RB_RED, RB_BLACK } color;
} rbnode_t;

struct rbtree {
    rbnode_t *root;           /* == nil when empty */
    rbnode_t *nil;            /* shared black sentinel leaf; not counted in size */
    size_t size;
    rb_value_free_fn value_free;
};

content.text:
Here's how both would look, using this codebase's actual struct (sentinel `t->nil` instead of `NULL` for leaves):

## Recursive

A small `static` helper does the real work; the public function just kicks it off.

```c
static void rb_foreach_node(const rbtree_t *t, rbnode_t *node,
                             void (*fn)(const char *, void *, void *),
                             void *ctx)
{
    if (node == t->nil)
        return;
    rb_foreach_node(t, node->left, fn, ctx);
    fn(node->key, node->value, ctx);
    rb_foreach_node(t, node->right, fn, ctx);
}

void rb_foreach(const rbtree_t *t,
                void (*fn)(const char *key, void *value, void *ctx),
                void *ctx)
{
    rb_foreach_node(t, t->root, fn, ctx);
}
```

Straightforward, mirrors the textbook definition of in-order (left, visit, right). The downside: each call frame holds `node`/return address, so a pathological or very deep tree risks stack overflow — although a red-black tree's height is bounded at `O(log n)`, so in practice this is safe for any realistic `n`.

## Iterative

No recursion needed, but you need an explicit stack of ancestor nodes to know where to go after finishing a subtree.

```c
void rb_foreach(const rbtree_t *t,
                void (*fn)(const char *key, void *value, void *ctx),
                void *ctx)
{
    rbnode_t **stack = rb_malloc(sizeof(rbnode_t *) * (something sized to height));
    size_t top = 0;
    rbnode_t *node = t->root;

    /* invariant: everything below `node` not yet visited is reachable
       either via `node` itself or via a parent saved on the stack */
    while (node != t->nil || top > 0) {
        while (node != t->nil) {
            stack[top++] = node;
            node = node->left;
        }
        node = stack[--top];
        fn(node->key, node->value, ctx);
        node = node->right;
    }

    rb_free(stack);
}
```

This avoids recursion depth entirely but introduces a heap allocation (or a fixed-size array sized to the tree's max height) that itself needs a NULL check per this project's allocation rules — an annoyance the recursive version doesn't have, since C's call stack does that bookkeeping for free.

## Which fits this lab

Given `rb_foreach` has no failure mode in the header contract (`void` return, no error code), adding a `rb_malloc` call here means inventing an allocation-failure story `rb_foreach` isn't documented to have. The recursive version needs no allocation and matches the header's simplicity — I'd default to that unless you have a specific reason (e.g., avoiding recursion for grading/style reasons) to want the iterative version.
